**Class Subjects** is where a grade template becomes one class's reality: *this* class studies these
subjects, taught by these people. It is the last thing the timetable generator needs.

**You need:** a [grade curriculum applied](/docs/educonnect-tutorial-grade-curriculum) to the class,
and [teachers who list the subject](/docs/educonnect-tutorial-add-a-teacher) among the ones they teach.

## Steps

1. Open **Timetable → Class Subjects**.
2. Pick the **class** at the top. If you applied the grade curriculum, its subjects are already
   listed with no teacher yet. If you see **No subjects assigned to *class***, either the curriculum
   was never applied to that grade, or the class's grade is not what you think it is.
3. For each row, set the **teacher**. Only teachers who list that subject appear in the picker — if
   the person you want is missing, add the subject to their teacher record first.
4. To add a subject the curriculum did not include, select **Add Subject** and fill in:
   - **Subject** and **teacher**,
   - **Lessons/week**, **Max consec.** and **Max/day** — all optional. Left blank they inherit the
     grade curriculum's numbers; filled in, the row shows the value with **(override)** next to it,
     and that class alone gets the different treatment.
5. Save each row as you go. **Remove** takes an assignment off the class after confirming.

## Overrides, and when to use them

Override at the class level only for genuine exceptions — a class that gets an extra Maths period,
a split group that only meets twice a week. Anything that applies to the whole grade belongs in
[Grade Curriculum](/docs/educonnect-tutorial-grade-curriculum) instead, where it stays consistent
across classes and survives the next **Apply to Grade**.

## What to do next

Once every class has subjects with teachers against them, you have everything the generator needs:
[Generate the Timetable](/docs/educonnect-tutorial-generate-timetable).
